  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn active weather pattern \nwill persist across SEAK through at least early next week as \nmultiple fronts/lows will push through the Gulf and into the \npanhandle. The big player that will send the disturbances into \nthe Gulf and through SEAK will be the remnants of Typhoon Dujuan \nand associated upper-level low/trough that will slowly swing \nthrough southern interior of AK and into the Gulf through early \nnext week. Additionally, in between each front, cooler, moist \nonshore flow will keep mostly cloudy skies and showers. A frontal \nband will be sliding east across the panhandle Sunday morning, \nwith a period of moderate to heavy rain and gusty S-SEly winds. By\nSunday night into Monday, a low/front will will pivot around the \nperiphery of the parent upper-low and lift northeast into the Gulf\nand strengthen as it approaches the panhandle, with another surge\nof moderate to heavy rain and gusty winds. This system is \nstronger and will tap into a deeper feed of Pacific moisture, with\nmoderate to heavy rain expected. Some guidance is quite \naggressive in deepening the low and bringing gales into the Gulf \nand into Clarence Strait. In addition, cooler temperatures aloft \nassociated with the upper trough will result in modest \ninstability, bringing a potential for isolated thunderstorms, \nprimarily along the Gulf Coast and southern panhandle. While \nprecipitation rates could become heavy, individual fronts remain \nrelatively progressive, keeping the threat of prolonged, \ncontinuous heavy rainfall and localized flooding lower. \nPrecipitation probability guidance showing a slight potential of \nrainfall rates exceeding 6-hour or 24-hour 2-year return intervals\nwith each system, with northern panhandle favored for heaviest \nrain amounts on Sunday (10-15% of exceeding 2 year return \nintervals), and southern panhandle favored for Sunday night/Monday\nsystem (10-20%). For Tuesday into Wednesday, rain chances and \nprecip rates are favored to be on a downward as the primary Gulf \nlow weakens and the feed of deeper moisture shifts southeast of \nthe panhandle towards BC. However, quite a bit of model \ndiscrepancy remains for the period as some guidance is suggesting \nweak ridging building in by Wednesday. For now, kept mostly cloudy\nskies and scattered showers over SEAK with the upper trough and \nweaker onshore flow favored to persist. The rainy and cloudy \npattern will keep temperatures seasonably cool with highs \ngenerally in the low to mid 50s.",
